ntial chow and if I keep B2, I can wait for B4. Let's see:\n\nDiscard B3. Now, I have B2 in hand. If I can draw B4, then form B234 chow. That would help in forming another meld. Also, the unshown B4 has 4 tiles left, so there's a good chance of getting it.\n\nAnother option: discard T3. Then I have T2 left. If I can get T4, then T234 chow. But T4 has 3 unshown. So that's also possible.\n\nBut which is better? If I discard B3 or T3. Which one gives me more flexibility?\n\nAlternatively, maybe discard W6. Since I have only one W6, and it's not part of any obvious chow. But if I discard W6, someone might take it for a chow. But since W5 is gone, W6 can be part of W678, but I don't have those. The previous player has a W456 chow, so maybe someone else has W6 in their hand. But if I discard W6, they might take it. But since W5 is gone, maybe not.\n\nHmm. Considering all possibilities, maybe the best is to discard B3 to help form the B234 chow. So the answer would be Play B3.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The optimal move is to discard B3 to facilitate forming a B234 chow if B4 is drawn, while preserving B2 for potential completion. This maintains flexibility in the Dots suit and avoids giving others key tiles for their melds.\nReason: Discarding B3 allows retaining B2 to pair with potential B4 for a chow, aligning with the existing Dots melds. It also reduces the risk of others completing their own Dots chows, as B3 is less critical than B2 for sequential melds. The unavailability of W5 makes W4/W6 chows unfeasible, so focusing on Dots or Bamboos is more viable.\nAnswer: Play B3"}
