ard. Unless they can form a pair of W5 and W6, but that's not possible. \\n\\nIf they play W5, the hand becomes [W4, W4, W6, T1]. Then they can't form a meld with those. \\n\\nIf they play W6, the hand is [W4, W4, W5, T1]. Again, no meld. \\n\\nIf they play T1, the hand is [W4, W4, W5, W6]. Then they can form a Chow of W4-W5-W6 and a pair of W4. That would work. \\n\\nSo the best move is to play T1, which leaves the hand with W4, W4, W5, W6. Then they can form a Chow and a pair. \\n\\nBut wait, the valid actions are Play T1, W5, W6, W4. So the user can choose to play T1. That would allow them to have the necessary tiles for the fourth meld. \\n\\nAlternatively, if they play W4, they have one W4 left. Then the hand is [W4, W5, W6, T1]. They can't form a meld here. \\n\\nSo the best option is to play T1. \\n\\nBut let me check the unshown tiles. T1 has 1 left. If they play T1, someone else might need it. But if the user needs T1 for their own hand, maybe it's better to keep it. Wait, in the user's current hand, T1 is a lone tile. If they keep it, they can't form a meld. So playing T1 is necessary to form the fourth meld. \\n\\nSo the answer is to play T1.\\n\", \"output\": \"Evaluation: Playing T1 allows the completion of a Chow (W4, W5, W6) and a pair of W4, forming a winning hand with four melds and a pair. Other plays risk losing the ability to form the necessary melds.\\nReason: Discarding T1 enables the formation of a W4-W5-W6 Chow and a W4 pair, completing the fourth meld required for victory. Keeping T1 would leave the hand incomplete, while discarding W5 or W6 could disrupt potential melds.\\nAnswer: Play T1\"}"}
